From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: retry PCIe firmware bring-up before giving up

This machine's BCM4387 (Wi-Fi/BT combo chip) intermittently crashes
during brcmfmac's PCIe firmware bring-up -- drivers/net/wireless/
broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c's brcmf_pcie_setup() logs "Dongle
setup failed" at its `fail:` label and, on the very first failure,
calls brcmf_fw_crashed() then device_release_driver(), permanently
unbinding the driver until something reloads the module. The crash
signature (THREADX TRAP address, stack contents) varies between
occurrences, consistent with a timing race rather than a deterministic
bug, and it is not root-caused upstream: it is not the already-fixed
BCM4387 AXI-reset issue (brcmf_chip_cr4_set_passive() in chip.c already
only calls brcmf_chip_coredisable(), not a reset release, matching
Hector Martin's existing fix), and it originates in Broadcom's
closed-source on-chip firmware, which isn't decodable from this driver.
A NixOS-level userspace workaround already reloads the module after
boot if it never binds (see brcmfmac-wifi-crash-recovery.nix), but a
kernel-level fix is worth doing directly in the driver's own failure
path instead of only relying on userspace to notice.

Give brcmf_pcie_setup() a bounded number of attempts (3) before
unbinding. Each attempt, including the first, starts with a hardware
watchdog reset of the chip via the existing brcmf_pcie_reset_device()
(the same targeted reset the runtime brcmf_pcie_reset() bus op already
uses for crash recovery once the device is up) rather than assuming the
chip resets itself to a clean slate on its own -- this is cheaper and
more targeted than brcmf_pcie_remove()'s full teardown, which frees
devinfo/bus and can't be used mid-probe. On failure, whatever
host-side state this attempt reached (IRQ, scratch buffers, ring
buffers) is released -- each release function is already individually
guarded by its own allocated/initialized flag or a NULL check, so this
is safe regardless of how far a given attempt got -- and the whole
firmware request is re-issued from scratch via
brcmf_pcie_prepare_fw_request() + brcmf_fw_get_firmwares(), the same
pair of calls brcmf_pcie_reset() uses. brcmf_detach() is deliberately
not called here (unlike brcmf_pcie_reset()): nothing has attached yet
at any failure point inside brcmf_pcie_setup(), since the only
brcmf_attach() call in this function is itself one of the goto-fail
sites. The retry counter lives on brcmf_pciedev_info and resets to
zero on a successful bring-up, so a crash recurring hours into an
uptime session (e.g. after suspend/resume) gets its own fresh 3
attempts rather than inheriting a stale count from boot.

Incidentally fixes a small pre-existing leak on the request_irq
failure path: every other goto-fail site in this function either
releases the fw/fwsig/nvram firmware blobs itself or has already
handed them to brcmf_pcie_download_fw_nvram() (which frees them
unconditionally, even on its own failure), but the request_irq failure
check -- running before download_fw_nvram -- jumped to `fail:`
without releasing them. Harmless as a one-time leak, but worth fixing
now that a retry loop would otherwise repeat it on every attempt.

Signed-off-by: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -427,6 +427,7 @@
 	struct brcmf_mp_device *settings;
 	struct brcmf_otp_params otp;
 	bool fwseed;
+	unsigned int setup_attempts;
 #ifdef DEBUG
 	u32 console_interval;
 	bool console_active;
@@ -2495,6 +2496,22 @@
 #define BRCMF_PCIE_FW_TXCAP	3
 #define BRCMF_PCIE_FW_SIG	4
 
+/*
+ * The firmware crash this chip (BCM4387) intermittently hits during PCIe
+ * bring-up (see the "retrying dongle setup" / "Dongle setup failed" paths
+ * below) has no known host-side root cause -- it originates in Broadcom's
+ * closed-source on-chip firmware and isn't decodable from this driver.
+ * Rather than give up on the first failure, retry the whole bring-up a
+ * bounded number of times: each retry does a hardware watchdog reset of
+ * the chip (brcmf_pcie_reset_device(), the same targeted reset already used
+ * by the runtime brcmf_pcie_reset() bus op -- not the much heavier
+ * brcmf_pcie_remove(), which frees devinfo/bus and can't be used mid-probe)
+ * before re-requesting firmware and re-running setup from scratch. This
+ * mirrors brcmf_pcie_reset()'s own recovery sequence, minus brcmf_detach()
+ * since nothing has attached yet at any failure point in this function.
+ */
+#define BRCMF_PCIE_SETUP_MAX_ATTEMPTS	3
+
 static void brcmf_pcie_setup(struct device *dev, int ret,
 			     struct brcmf_fw_request *fwreq)
 {
@@ -2514,6 +2531,15 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto fail;
 
+	/*
+	 * Always bring the chip up from a known hardware state before
+	 * touching it, on the first attempt as well as on retries -- don't
+	 * assume it reset itself cleanly on its own. This is the same
+	 * watchdog reset brcmf_pcie_reset() already relies on for runtime
+	 * recovery.
+	 */
+	brcmf_pcie_reset_device(devinfo);
+
 	brcmf_pcie_attach(devinfo);
 
 	fw = fwreq->items[BRCMF_PCIE_FW_CODE].binary;
@@ -2545,8 +2571,20 @@
 	 */
 	brcmf_pcie_select_core(devinfo, BCMA_CORE_PCIE2);
 	ret = brcmf_pcie_request_irq(devinfo);
-	if (ret)
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * Pre-existing leak on this specific failure path (every
+		 * other goto-fail site below this point either releases
+		 * fw/fwsig/nvram itself or has already handed them to
+		 * brcmf_pcie_download_fw_nvram(), which frees them
+		 * unconditionally). Fix it here too, since the retry loop
+		 * added below would otherwise compound it on every attempt.
+		 */
+		release_firmware(fw);
+		release_firmware(fwsig);
+		brcmf_fw_nvram_free(nvram);
 		goto fail;
+	}
 
 	ret = brcmf_pcie_download_fw_nvram(devinfo, fw, fwsig, nvram, nvram_len);
 	if (ret)
@@ -2591,11 +2629,42 @@
 
 	brcmf_pcie_fwcon_timer(devinfo, true);
 
+	devinfo->setup_attempts = 0;
 	return;
 
 fail:
 	brcmf_err(bus, "Dongle setup failed\n");
 	brcmf_pcie_bus_console_read(devinfo, true);
+
+	if (++devinfo->setup_attempts < BRCMF_PCIE_SETUP_MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
+		struct brcmf_fw_request *retry_fwreq;
+
+		brcmf_err(bus,
+			  "retrying dongle setup after firmware crash (attempt %u/%u)\n",
+			  devinfo->setup_attempts + 1, BRCMF_PCIE_SETUP_MAX_ATTEMPTS);
+
+		/*
+		 * Tear down whatever host-side state this attempt got to
+		 * (all of these are no-ops/idempotent if that step was never
+		 * reached -- each is individually guarded by its own
+		 * allocated/initialized flag or a NULL check). Nothing has
+		 * attached yet at any failure point above, so unlike
+		 * brcmf_pcie_reset() there is no brcmf_detach() to do here.
+		 */
+		brcmf_pcie_release_irq(devinfo);
+		brcmf_pcie_release_scratchbuffers(devinfo);
+		brcmf_pcie_release_ringbuffers(devinfo);
+		brcmf_pcie_reset_device(devinfo);
+
+		retry_fwreq = brcmf_pcie_prepare_fw_request(devinfo);
+		if (retry_fwreq &&
+		    !brcmf_fw_get_firmwares(dev, retry_fwreq, brcmf_pcie_setup))
+			return;
+
+		brcmf_err(bus, "failed to re-arm firmware request for retry\n");
+		kfree(retry_fwreq);
+	}
+
 	brcmf_fw_crashed(dev);
 	device_release_driver(dev);
 }
